WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.306 --> 00:00:07.589
(airplane propellers starting)

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:11.240
(dramatic music)

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.640
- It's absolutely deafening
inside this cockpit.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.670
You have an engine that is
screaming loud in front of you.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.960
The cockpit's 140 degrees inside.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:21.980
Everything is shaking,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.733
vibrating in its own different way.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.910
Why would you wanna do
something like this?

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.433
Bottom line is every
pilot wants to go fast.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.080
- Steve Hinton is trying
to set the fastest speed

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.550
ever accomplished with a propeller-driven

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.270
piston engine aircraft.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.580
- To break this record
you have to be perfect.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.230
This is the pinnacle of aviation.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.270
- Steve's dad broke the
record at 499 miles an hour.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.760
- [Steve] There's only few
people that have ever stood

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.090
on top of this mountain.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.700
The Wright brothers, Jimmy D. Little,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.640
Glenn Curtiss, Howard Hughes.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.690
- Just these icons of aviation.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.140
- And Steve believes that
he is the man to do it.

00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:04.200
- 535 is the magic number.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.442
If we go 535 miles an hour,
that's a new world record.

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:10.192
(dramatic music)

00:01:17.095 --> 00:01:18.903
- [Announcer] Hold your
position, we got an emergency.

00:01:19.895 --> 00:01:21.190
(sirens blaring)

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.960
- My dad was definitely a hero of mine.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.400
He also air raced, he
set world speed records,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.140
and won several races with an airplane

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.270
called the Red Baron.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.783
And he's alive to tell the tale about it.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.240
- [Reporter] At the scene of the crash

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.190
medics have recovered Steve Hinton

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.203
from the shattered cockpit of the Baron.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.870
- This is serious business,
and I've been severely hurt,

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.770
I broke my back and broke my legs

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.440
when I crashed the Red Baron for instance,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.560
but I was young and I healed well.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.210
- [Reporter] And now
this MedEvac helicopter

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.210
is the key to survival for Steve Hinton.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.920
- You know, it's funny being
part of an aviation family.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.220
When Steve, Sr. crashed
crashed the Red Baron

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.500
it never even occurred to
me to say, "Don't fly."

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.023
You can't separate
someone from their soul.

00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:10.384
(slow music)

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.370
- Fifteen years ago was the first time

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.883
I really got bit by this air racing bug.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.610
I dug through my parents' attic and found

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.620
a bunch of old photographs of my dad

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.420
from his racing days and found his trophy,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.903
which he got for setting
the world speed record.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.280
He held that record for 10
years until Lyle Shelton

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.180
broke that same record
at 528 miles an hour.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.130
It is still considered one of
the hardest records to break.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.950
The allure, it's just kinda pulled me in;

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.923
it's like this gravitational force.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.430
But having an opportunity
to break a speed record

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.213
I thought was beyond a long shot.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.340
Our family operates a warbird museum

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.810
based in Chino, California,
that is dedicated

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.083
to preserving, maintaining,
flying these aircraft.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:28.190
My sister and I had a crib
in my mother's office here

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.130
while she was doing the books.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.470
And some of my earliest memories

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:33.890
are waddling out into the hanger

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.940
and seeing my dad working on an aircraft.

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:40.156
(slow music)

00:03:43.075 --> 00:03:44.760
- You know, he was probably a baby

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.030
sittin' out next to the toolbox

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.030
when his dad was takin' an engine apart.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.710
Before he could talk he
probably was already gettin' it.

00:03:55.063 --> 00:03:57.813
- The warbirds, so to speak,
are special in our family.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.100
We are really intrigued with
the mechanical end of 'em,

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:02.933
the history.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.850
They were built for war,
they're built for combat,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.000
and they're built for killing.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.803
But there were the Apollo
spaceships in their day.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.403
- [Steve] There is a mystique
that surrounds these aircraft.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.770
There was no computers that built 'em;

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.450
they were all hand-built.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.800
Calculated with slide
rulers, pencils, and paper.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.120
- To be able to understand
how the entire aircraft works

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.480
is just a really important
part of being a pilot

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.640
of a World War II aircraft.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.240
Steve had always grown
up with that expectation

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.140
that you'll really know your
airplane when you work on it.

00:04:41.432 --> 00:04:44.050
- [Steve, Sr.] And once he started racing,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.000
he started winning.

00:04:50.042 --> 00:04:52.625
(upbeat music)

00:04:54.134 --> 00:04:57.040
- I was able to win my first
year out at the age of 22,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.870
which bested my dad's record of being

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.600
the youngest to win an air race.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.160
And then I was lucky enough to win

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.943
seven of eight races over
the last eight years.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:06.813
- [Steve, Sr.] And that was a big deal,

00:05:06.813 --> 00:05:08.320
he won his first four big races.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.273
He -- won and beat everybody
handily without any trouble.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.810
- It was just this total Cinderella story.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.010
How many men have tried
to win the unlimited gold

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.270
and have never even won a heat race?

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.680
- There's guys that have
done this for 40 years

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.690
and only won seven times.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.700
I felt like the last way to
leave your impression on a sport

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.130
is to go after the speed record,

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.100
because once this breed
of airplane is extinct

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.603
it won't be capable of being broken again.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.210
For this record attempt we've
come to a private ranch,

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.570
which our sponsor, Joe Clark, owns,

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.103
which is an ideal location for the record.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.560
He's got his own runway
on it, and he's given us

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.560
the opportunity to try and make history.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.120
- There's some better
places to do this at,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.460
but the runway isn't long
and the terrain is horrible.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.550
There are literally an
infinite amount of areas here

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.230
that you could safely land this airplane

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:21.730
on its belly and not get hurt.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.763
- If the engine blows he's
gotta get it on the ground.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:29.820
We got a lot of runway here,

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.073
and we have overruns at both ends.

00:06:33.004 --> 00:06:35.520
(airplane engine roaring)

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.530
It's gonna take a lot of skill,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.730
and concentration, and machinery.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.130
I mean this machine's gonna be runnin'

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.730
at 3,600 horsepower,

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.790
and there's only maybe two
or three other airplanes

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.290
in the whole world that
can do this at this level.

00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:53.650
(slow music)

00:06:59.698 --> 00:07:01.480
- The aircraft that we're
using to break this record

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.520
is what's called a North
American P-51 Mustang,

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:06.990
and we've named it Voodoo.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.203
P-51 changed the war.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:14.740
The aerodynamics of the airplane were such

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.703
that it was far superior to
many other aircraft around it.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:22.870
- [Steve, Sr.] You might
say it's an old warbird,

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.450
and, you know, there's new
airplanes that can do this,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:25.700
and that's just not true.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.740
That's as good as it's gonna get.

00:07:33.315 --> 00:07:34.710
- [Steve] It's a small
air frame, which means

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.810
it's shaped like a
bullet; it's real quick,

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.220
and it really has lent itself into being

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.883
a nice racing platform.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:50.883
- [Steve, Sr.] Thank you.

00:07:50.883 --> 00:07:51.716
- You're welcome.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.270
My dad and I aren't
emotionally close people;

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.270
it's a very business-type
of a relationship.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.594
We've talked about mechanical
aspects and how things work,

00:08:01.594 --> 00:08:03.810
but as far as how would you do
this, how would you do that,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.060
I haven't gotten much
advice from him at all.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.940
He thought it would be
more beneficial for me

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.903
to experience my own thought process.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.140
- They don't have to say
"I love you" every day.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:22.040
The fact that they work
together on things is the bond

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:26.283
and it's kinda the glue
that holds us together.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:31.540
- I know you know this,
but I'll say it anyway,

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.998
the time of day makes a big difference,

00:08:33.998 --> 00:08:35.660
and, you know, it's like
the -- with the clouds,

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.330
but the ground looks different every time,

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.740
so that's why the more flying the better.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:40.730
- We're about two o'clock right now,

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:44.110
and we're gonna be doin' it
between two, three, four, five,

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:45.260
somewhere in there, so.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.810
- [Steve, Sr.] He's assuming
a lot of responsibility,

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.970
which is a lot of load.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.160
I've kept an eye on 'im quite a bit.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.260
He's handling it well,
but I think it's adding

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.310
to the stress level that he doesn't need.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.723
- Prior to flying the airplane
I'm 110 percent mechanic.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.560
The big key to us breaking this record

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.560
will be the aerodynamics of the airplane

00:09:13.822 --> 00:09:15.780
and the design is so solid that we can

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.770
push the limits a little bit more

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:19.270
than what it was designed for.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.840
Every aspect of this airplane
is so far beyond the envelope

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.400
it was originally intended to do.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.303
Because of that, the engine
is commonly breaking.

00:09:34.729 --> 00:09:38.100
- This engine is going to
be completely stressed.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.290
It's going to be flying at
power longer than it has to

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:42.353
during an air raid.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.010
- There's a tremendous
amount of maintenance

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:46.060
that goes on with it.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.574
That includes test flying at full power.

00:09:50.574 --> 00:09:53.830
(dramatic music)

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.160
- It's probably the most dangerous part

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.610
of this record is the engine.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.380
What you're trying to do is get it to go

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.913
to the absolute maximum without breaking.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.900
- There's this fuse,
once we start opening up

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:10.260
the throttle on this
engine, that fuse gets lit.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.810
We don't know now long the fuse is.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.510
No one knows how long the fuse is.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:17.320
It's a lot of passion.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:20.510
You have mechanical failures,
you have lots of late nights.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.020
You gotta always try and see that light

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.920
at the end of the tunnel, even
though you may not see it,

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.870
you have to have faith that it's there.

00:10:38.003 --> 00:10:39.817
- [Man] Looking good.

00:10:39.817 --> 00:10:42.393
- Should be starting up here
in the next half an hour.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.390
Obviously, the first priority
is that this is a safe flight.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.340
We're obviously going for a speed record,

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:51.335
but the priority is to
be on the ground safely.

00:10:51.335 --> 00:10:52.550
So, if the airplane does have some sort

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.340
of an emergency or Mayday,

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:57.190
we ask that everybody just
stay away from the runway.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.995
I'm not gonna swerve to miss
ya, so, get off the runway.

00:10:59.995 --> 00:11:03.495
(laughing and applauding)

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.010
- Pilots have been
looking at the old record

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.420
for many, many years waiting

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.870
for someone to come and break it.

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:23.290
To do it he must fly over
this course four times,

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:24.870
two times in each direction

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.570
to come up with a final average speed.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.853
He must exceed the current
record by one percent.

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.860
- My job today is to be the chase plane.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.200
If I see something that doesn't look right

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.440
I'll be able to watch him and help him,

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.077
so I'll be running the shoulder with 'im.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.890
- If we're lucky we'll get
one crack at this record.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.950
If we're not successful we
are really rolling the dice

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.395
to try and set this thing again.

00:11:56.395 --> 00:11:59.145
(dramatic music)

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.790
- [Joe] These engines are
like people: they talk to you,

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.511
and in this particular
case it's gotta last

00:12:04.511 --> 00:12:06.313
probably 10, 15 minutes.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.710
- [Steve] Once we start
opening up the throttle

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:12.973
on this engine it could go
within the first 45 seconds.

00:12:23.114 --> 00:12:25.496
- [Man] Get off the runway!
- [Man] Out of the runway!

00:12:25.496 --> 00:12:27.712
- [Woman] Get off the runway!

00:12:27.712 --> 00:12:29.957
- [Woman] Get off the runway, now!

00:12:29.957 --> 00:12:31.033
- [Man] Get off the runway,
hey, off the runway!

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.230
- I thought I heard off
power, but I'm not sure,

00:12:35.122 --> 00:12:39.453
and I don't know what that means.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.390
- So we were just settin'
up for making that attempt

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:13.260
on the speed record and climbed out,

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.401
got to altitude, started
coming up on power,

00:13:15.401 --> 00:13:19.340
everything was going nice,
got the course in sight,

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.520
threw up on full power
for about 10 or 15 seconds

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:25.100
and got a little sneeze,
like a -- and then a boom,

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:26.890
and it blew a bunch of shit
out of the exhaust stacks,

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:31.080
so we declared a Mayday, and
cruised on in and landed.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.060
It was still making power
just a lot of oil pressure.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:36.250
So we don't know what the
issue is at the moment.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.780
There is coolant all over the
right side of the airplane,

00:13:39.731 --> 00:13:41.350
so we will go back to work and see

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.865
if we can figure out what's happening

00:13:43.865 --> 00:13:45.580
and how to get her back in the sky.

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:46.413
- [Cameraman] Thanks, man.

00:13:46.413 --> 00:13:47.246
- Yeah.

00:13:48.728 --> 00:13:49.561
Fuck!

00:13:52.969 --> 00:13:55.386
(slow music)

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:04.550
- Crew's gonna have to look it over

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:06.033
and determine what happened.

00:14:07.528 --> 00:14:09.530
It could be major or it
could be something simple,

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.080
we don't know at this point;

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:13.543
but the airplane's here, Steve's here,

00:14:15.226 --> 00:14:18.043
so if it just needs a part or
something, we'll find a way.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:24.810
- You really have to be
ready for those punches

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:26.440
to get thrown at you in this industry

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.653
because it is 75-year-old equipment,

00:14:30.232 --> 00:14:31.782
but I'm so proud of these guys.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.670
Normal people wouldn't do
the stuff that we're doing,

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:38.420
they would've quit a long time ago.

00:14:39.856 --> 00:14:42.273
(slow music)

00:14:54.447 --> 00:14:56.864
(slow music)

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.230
- [Steve, Sr.] It's not natural to fly,

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:10.480
I don't care who you are.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:13.760
You have to get experience.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.060
Understanding limitations and
understanding consequences.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:22.190
Unfortunately, we've had friends
who aren't with us anymore,

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.053
and Steve has gone through that.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:30.250
- As a mother you can't go through

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:32.683
these kinds of things unaffected.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:36.170
You vibrate with every core of your being

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:38.053
during these experiences.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.800
I don't wanna watch him do this again.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.840
And I know that's what they do.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.040
They blow the engine, they
change it, and they go again,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:52.233
and it's just not gonna
be very easy to watch.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.210
- Alrighty, if you like, I like it.

00:15:59.168 --> 00:16:02.751
(airplane engine starting)

00:16:04.810 --> 00:16:07.120
The pilot has to fly perfect,

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:09.150
the airframe has to work perfect,

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.973
the engine needs to run perfectly.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.783
Everything has to come
together for it to be perfect.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:20.810
We're not doing this
record for somebody else;

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.140
we're not doing this for
another group of people;

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:26.550
we're not trying to prove that
we're faster than anybody;

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.773
we're doing this for ourselves.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:32.253
Okay, I'm gonna start coming over the --.

00:16:33.702 --> 00:16:35.188
- [Steve, Sr.] OK, clock is rollin'.

00:16:35.188 --> 00:16:36.605
- [Steve] Got it.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:48.210
It's absolutely deafening
inside this cockpit.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.913
You have an engine that is
screaming loud in front of you.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:55.870
There's 18 different gauges in the cockpit

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:57.663
that you're trying to keep track of.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.450
In the midst of all this chaos

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:05.260
you talk to the engine,
talk to the airplane,

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:08.580
it's you and the piece of
equipment, you and the machine.

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:11.360
OK, I got -- on it, -- leave it there.

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:12.950
It's been an all consuming project

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.370
for the last four to five years.

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:17.400
All this work throughout the year,

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.823
all of these moments of dreaming,

00:17:21.172 --> 00:17:23.203
it essentially comes down to 30 seconds.

00:17:27.617 --> 00:17:29.570
- [Man] He's blowing
oil; he's losing power.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:30.630
- [Man] Yup.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.040
- [Man] Check the oil pressure.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.530
- [Steve] Yeah, I'm gonna
turn her back around here.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.020
The first thing in your mind is,

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:38.600
come on, you can hold together,

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:40.376
there's only a little bit left.

00:17:40.376 --> 00:17:41.600
Alright, last lap, c'mon, Baby.

00:17:43.417 --> 00:17:45.834
(slow music)

00:18:11.055 --> 00:18:15.510
On the one hand it was successful,
we're back on the ground.

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:17.070
Between the third and fourth pass

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:19.270
the oil pressure started falling off,

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:20.750
so it'll be interesting
to see how the speeds

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.253
reflect the four passes.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:24.530
We'll wait for about an hour

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:28.390
until Brain Utley from the
NAA does his calculations

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:31.660
and makes sure I didn't break
any altitude restrictions,

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:33.170
and then we'll get an
average of the four laps

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:34.120
and see what we got.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:45.643
Everybody together!

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.250
- Let me walk you through the numbers,

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:56.210
'cause I think it's important
to know the numbers.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:59.160
The old record, 528.33 --

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.119
- You said "old record," so
that means there's new record.

00:19:02.119 --> 00:19:05.619
(laughing and applauding)

00:19:09.397 --> 00:19:14.313
- You must exceed the current
record by one percent.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:19.083
So with the first lap, the first pass,

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:25.140
Steven averaged 554.69 miles an hour,

00:19:26.016 --> 00:19:27.913
absolutely fantastic.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:32.683
On the second pass he averaged 527.34

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:39.713
for an average over the two passes of 541.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:46.350
On the third pass he
averaged 528.48 miles an hour

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:53.493
for a three pass average of 536.84.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.720
On the fourth pass, unfortunately,

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:02.720
he only averaged 515.6 miles an hour,

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:06.150
which dropped his overall
average down to 531,

00:20:08.316 --> 00:20:10.543
and I'm sorry I can't
give you better news,

00:20:11.759 --> 00:20:14.653
but, through the third lap, it was there.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:21.267
But congratulations for a
great, great, great flight.

00:20:21.267 --> 00:20:23.684
(applauding)

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:31.910
- [Steve] It's surreal when
he, you know, told us the speed

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:32.743
and this is the average.

00:20:32.743 --> 00:20:34.833
It's like, huh, that's it.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.510
The end.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:41.030
No fairy tale ending, no
fireworks, no celebration,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:42.430
it's just kind of this, huh.

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:47.696
- This engine ran real well,

00:20:47.696 --> 00:20:52.696
but it unfortunately missed
the beat by about 30 seconds.

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:58.340
We do our best to figure
all the variables,

00:20:58.340 --> 00:20:59.503
but that's racing.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:06.610
- Before the fourth pass the
oil pressure began fluctuating

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:08.910
and these engines, when they
start losing oil pressure,

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:12.080
something is gonna
explode, and I was faced

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:15.410
with this decision of do
I go out, do I come back,

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:17.020
so I just quickly turned
the airplane around,

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:19.470
but I could feel that it
bled a lot of speed off.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:22.840
I know the limitations of the airplane,

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:23.787
I know what I can get away with,

00:21:23.787 --> 00:21:25.260
and I know what I can't get away with,

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:28.563
and this was a decision that
was right on the edge of that.

00:21:35.878 --> 00:21:38.461
(upbeat music)

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:52.200
It's a bit hard going to sleep at night

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.020
'cause it'll keep me up.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:55.720
You know, you always go
through the what ifs,

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.260
so I tend to try not to think about it,

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:02.193
and hopefully there will be
a next chapter at some point.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.530
- You know, it's like
just missing Everest.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:08.130
To set the record you have to beat

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:10.030
the previous record by one percent.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:12.480
Doesn't sound like a lot, but
when you're pushing the speeds

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.333
that these guys are pushing,
it's very difficult to do.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:21.580
- [Steve, Sr.] You know you
set a goal, and he went out

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:24.200
and executed it just like they'd planned

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:27.200
and, with any luck, Steve might
get another chance to do it.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:29.630
- We know where to improve the airplane;

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.970
we know where to improve the engine

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:33.540
and that gives us hope that if we do

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:34.580
get a chance to do this again,

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:37.433
we'll be able to break that
535 mile an hour number.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.670
As a matter of fact now we
have to go 536 miles an hour

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:43.040
'cause we have to break our
old record by one percent,

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:44.233
so here we go.

00:22:45.068 --> 00:22:47.568
(slow music)

